</div>

<p>There are a host of schools of thought on which direction is best to drive the whole of Route 66, but Attrell heads inland from the coast, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H0bA72fjHMQ">as travel vlogger Josh McNair&nbsp;did with his father</a> for his channel <em>Through My Lens</em> a few years before. They take only two weeks to do so, a fairly rapid clip at which to cross seven states, or eight if you count the tiny segment that passes through the southeast corner of Kansas.</p>
<p>httpv:// www.youtube.com/watch?v=H0bA72fjHMQ</p>
<p>There are, no doubt, Route 66 purists who would insist that no stretch, no matter how short, can be bypassed — or at least no stretch of the 85 percent of the original highway that remains drivable today. Route 66 was decommissioned in 1985, nearly three decades after the construction of the U.S. Interstate Highway System began making it obsolete. But it wasn’t long thereafter that efforts began to preserve as much of it as possible as Historic Route 66, an enterprise that may have initially been driven by nostalgia, but increasingly trades in the exoticism of the once-ordinary. It’s easy to over-romanticize the days of pre-Interstate automobile travel, but as the aesthetics and rhythms of life in that period pass out of living memory, their remains become all the more compelling, even in total desuetude.</p>
