<p>Anyone in search of total desuetude can hardly find more vivid examples, at least in my experience, than the Route 66 ghost towns (and, as Attrell calls them, “semi-ghost towns”) that the freeways passed by. For example,&nbsp;the strip of mostly hollowed-out lodgings that is <a href="https://laurinovak.com/a-town-left-behind-tucumcari-nm/">Tucumcari, New Mexico</a> has been a photographer’s dream for so long that a non-clichéd shot of the place can now be hard to come by. But for all the books published about it — and indeed, all the videos made about it — Route 66 is less a subject than an experience, a pilgrimage through an America of neon signs, period-accurate gas stations, memorabilia shops, eccentric artworks, <a href="https://www.cozydogdrivein.com/?srsltid=AfmBOopD5Y6HA37Al4gKEBSgtt-4aa00VsEqtoNaFyFy73H0QavTNWxw">hot dogs on sticks</a>, Muffler Man statues, diners both operating and abandoned, and, of course, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wigwam_Motel">wigwam motels</a>. May it endure to the United States’ quincentennial.</p>
